  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nEarlier discussion regarding this afternoon and evening's heavy\nrain threat (issued at 952 AM)...\n\nHave opted to issue a flood watch for portions of northern IL\ninto Lake County Indiana for this afternoon and evening. \n\nA cold front stretching from near Milwaukee to north central\nMissouri will move slowly southeastward through tonight. An\nunseasonably moist air mass is in place ahead of this front with\nan axis of 70F+ dewpoints noted pooling just ahead of the\nboundary across southeastern IA. The 12z ILX sounding had a PWAT\nof 1.77\" with SPC mesoanalysis depicting a corridor of 1.9-2.1\"\nPWATs pooling near the front from NW IL into SE IA and northern\nMO. This is about as moist of an air mass as can occur this time\nof year in this part of the country. \n\nAs the cold front moves slowly southeastward, a mid-level\nshortwave trough over northwest MO is expected to shear east-\nnortheastward into northern IL, providing enhanced ascent\ncoincident with the timing of the cold front moving into\nnorthern IL. The incredibly high dewpoints for early Oct should\ncontribute to some weak low level instability near and ahead of\nthe front despite the poor lapse rates. The weak low level\ninstability coupled with progged strong omega all below -10C,\nshould result in a very favorable warm rain process set-up,\nwhich tends to result in very efficient heavy rain production. \n\nGiven this afternoon's heavy rainfall looks to set up not far\nfrom where the axis of heaviest rainfall (>1\") fell yesterday,\nthere is increased concern for some flooding problems developing\nthis afternoon into this evening. The latest 3-hour flash flood\nguidance is around 1.75\", though naturally lower than that in\nthe highly urbanized areas of the Chicago metro area. There has\nbeen a very consistent signal in every run of the HRRR since 6z\nwith an axis 3 hour rainfall totals of 2.5 to nearly 4\" of rain\nthis afternoon ahead of this front, which is given the rather\nlow flash flood guidance, would be quite problematic.\n\nRemainder of the discussion (late this evening onward)...\n\nWhile the heavy rainfall threat is likely to diminish late this\nevening, some light rain may linger through midnight north of\nI-80 and only gradually end from the north south of I-80. It is\npossible that light rain may not end south down to the Hwy 24\ncorridor until just after sunrise Friday. \n\nOther than the chance of some lingering light rain near Hwy 24\nearly Friday morning, dry weather is expected through the\nweekend and potentially most of next week as well. Skies will\ngradually clear from north to south Friday. Despite increasingly\nsunny conditions, northerly winds behind the cold front should\nresult in cooler temperatures and noticeably less humid\nconditions Friday. \n\nClear skies and light winds should set the stage for the\nchilliest night of the fall for most Friday night. Widespread\n40s are expected outside of Chicago's urban heat island.\nCouldn't even totally rule out an isolated 39 in the typically\nchillier cold air drainage areas. \n\nA moisture starved cold front will sweep across the area Sunday.\nAfter a couple of days with highs in the 60s Friday and\nSaturday, it could briefly warm up into the lower 70s Sunday\nahead of the front. Behind the front, highs in the 60s are\nexpected the beginning of next week, gradually moderating back \ntoward 70 by midweek. All in all, dry and very pleasant fall \nweather is on tap after the rain ends tonight/very early Friday \nmorning. \n\n- Izzi",
